        "item_id": "EX-GRATING-01",
        "score": 5.17105748,
        "text": "# Check a grating order before moving a camera\n\nFor a transmission grating illuminated at normal incidence, use\n`m * wavelength = pitch * sin(theta)` to check whether a proposed diffraction\norder is physically possible. With a 1.5 micrometer pitch and 532 nm light,\nthe third order would require `sin(theta) = 1.064`, so it cannot propagate.\nThis check prevents a futile camera search.\n"
      },
      {
        "content_sha256": "35719a025fc1ec9a9da538d1bbf0f81aaa57d2e22f66bf18a2bc02f513d1a3e3",
        "estimated_tokens": 76,
        "item_id": "EX-DETECT-01",
        "score": 4.81120827,
        "text": "# Detector linearity calibration\n\nTo test whether a photodiode response is linear, attenuate a stable free-space\nbeam with calibrated neutral-density filters and record dark-subtracted output.\nFor an illustrative filter transmitting 12% of incident power, a linear response\nshould fall to about 12% of its previous dark-subtracted reading. If the ratio\nchanges with incident power, inspect amplifier saturation, offset, and detector\nbandwidth before interpreting the optical signal.\n\nRecord the filter transmission and dark offsets in the measurement log.\n"
      },
      {
        "content_sha256": "7163af8d9aacdca5116e780a8b2cf9d272826ec5a7777ce450293ee904abbe6a",
        "estimated_tokens": 69,
        "item_id": "EX-PINHOLE-01",
        "score": 2.63488246,
        "text": "# Spatial-filter alignment failure\n\nWhen a focused free-space beam misses a spatial-filter pinhole, first reduce\npower, place a viewing card at the focus, and adjust transverse pinhole position\nwith small steps. Distinguish clipping from a focus displaced along the optical\naxis: transverse motion changes the bright spot overlap, whereas axial motion\nchanges the spot size at the aperture. Do not infer that every low-throughput\ncondition calls for a smaller aperture.\n"
      }
    ],
